From 6b3a491f76faedbc0e855a2fbe609f31e3995188 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: fawad haider <153737+FawadHa1der@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 1 Oct 2026 12:22:42 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] wasm: task manager never creates a thread while holding
 m_mutex; dedicated tasks reuse parked threads (patch 0035)
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Under Emscripten, pthread_create from a pthread is a SYNCHRONOUS round trip to
the runtime's main JS thread, and the task manager made it while holding its
global m_mutex (enqueue_core -> spawn_dedicated_worker / spawn_worker; wait_for
-> spawn_worker; every handle_finished loop). The language server spawns a
.dedicated task for every LSP output message, request and request continuation:
~153 thread creations per edit cycle, measured. In the browser the runtime froze
for good about once per 4,400 edits — every Lean pthread stopped, the worker's
JS thread alive (qed64 docs/HARDENING.md #52; root cause by the widgets lane,
ROOT-CAUSE.md §4-5). One proxied round trip that never completed, made with
m_mutex held, stops every other thread at its next task-manager call.

Wasm only (LEAN_EMSCRIPTEN); the native branch is unchanged:
* No thread is created while m_mutex is held: spawn_worker /
  spawn_dedicated_worker drop the caller's lock around lthread construction (a
  std worker's slot is reserved first so every decision taken meanwhile counts
  it). Every caller of enqueue_core already tolerated an unlock (a sync task
  runs unlocked inside it).
* A dedicated task is handed to a PARKED dedicated thread when one exists; each
  parked thread waits on its own slot and takes exactly one task, so a
  dedicated task never waits behind another. At most 8 stay parked: a parked
  thread keeps its Emscripten Worker, which std workers cannot use, and 8 keeps
  application thread + full std pool + parked within the 24 preallocated
  Workers. Bursts beyond 8 still create threads (outside the lock).
* A reused thread looks fresh to its next task: the heartbeat limit a new
  thread would inherit travels with the task, and the thread-local std streams
  are reset to the process defaults (reset_thread_streams), outside the lock.
* resolve_core wakes the threads waiting on a finished task BEFORE handing off
  its dependents, which may now drop the lock for thread creations.
* wait_for's Task.get-from-a-sync-task panic prints (a proxied fd_write) with
  the lock released: nothing proxies to the main thread under m_mutex.
* lean_wasm_task_manager_parked_threads (lock-free export): parked threads are
  live pthreads, so a host gauge of live pthreads should subtract it.

Drafted by the QED64 session (cdfce380f22); reviewed by five independent
adversarial lenses (hand-off protocol proved: no lost/double hand-off, slot
lifetime, shutdown race; every lock-drop site; thread-locals; Emscripten
proxies; design), whose confirmed findings are the last four bullets and the
cap. Measured with wasm64-build/probes/task-storm.lean: the same storm creates
2,956 pthreads on the served runtime and 126 with the draft.

This narrows the exposure; it is not a cure if the lost wakeup is in the main
thread's mailbox itself: every LSP frame is still a synchronous fd_write proxy.

